        <section id="promoting-the-rights-of-individuals-with-autism"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Promoting the rights of individuals with autism</h2>
              <p>By promoting the rights of individuals with autism in everyday situations, using high profile ways such as campaigns and films, people can helped to increase their understanding.</p>
<p>The National Autistic Society set about doing this by creating a range of campaigns to inform about the rights of people with autism to be accepted and understood. Let’s look at a few campaigns that have endeavoured to do this.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          ‘Too Much Information’ campaign
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This campaign set out to challenge myths, misconceptions and stereotypes. This campaign ran for three years from 2015-2018 to help give the public a better understanding of autism and the rights of individuals. It focused on different  things such as extra time needed to process information, the anxiety felt in social situations and with unexpected changes. Also sensitivity to noise, smells and bright lights and how these can become overwhelming.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u2_s2_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Autism Hour
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This was launched in 2017 to encourage more businesses and public spaces to make small changes to become more autism friendly. This campaign supported the rights of individuals with autism to be able to use any space without being asked to leave because of behaviour associated with their needs. The small changes like lowering background noise, dimming lights, educating staff and sharing information with the public can help even if this is just for an hour.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u2_s2_p2_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Autism Friendly Award
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This award is given to premises that make sure autistic visitors receive the same welcome as everyone else. Small changes can make a massive difference to autistic visitors and just a little understanding can go a long way.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u2_s2_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Not Enough
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The ‘not enough’ campaign demands better support and services for autistic people and families across the country because things haven’t improved enough since the 2009 Autism Act promised changes.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/demo/ua_u2_s2_p2_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
