Case study</h2>
              <h3>Case study - Personalisation and needs led approach</h3>
<p>Personalisation means approaching support services in an entirely different way by looking at the individuals strengths, preferences and aspirations and putting them at the centre of the process, and making choices that reflect those preferences and needs. The traditional service-led approach has often meant that individuals with autism have not received the right help at the right time and have been unable to shape the kind of support they need.</p>
<p>Personalisation means everyone has access to the right information, advice and advocacy to make good decisions about the support they need and are able to access universal services such as transport, leisure and education, housing and health services and opportunities for employment.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Example - Mathew
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Mathew was a student with autism spectrum disorder that had a passion for games design. He had applied to go onto a games design course but due to struggles at high school had failed to achieve the grades needed. He was instead offered a route into a level 2 art qualification. He had always had a passion or obsession with drawing ‘dragons’ and always found a way of working them into the current project, showing little interest in other activities essential to passing the course.</p>
<p>There were many instances when Mathew wasn’t a fan of the subject matter or daily activity, with one particular example being manual print making with paints, he wasn’t interested and didn’t see any point in it. This however was a necessary stage of the course and needed to be completed. Mathew was helped to re-contextualise the task at hand. It was suggested he could look to his interest in dragons to reframe the task. His tutor showed him that by creating textures with paint and print techniques for the project, he could then scan these in and they could then be used as textures for the skin of his dragons and by being more experimental, he could create interesting backgrounds.</p>
<p>By being more open to trying things, Mathew was able to develop his own style and improve his confidence gaining the skills he needed to complete his course. He also was able to discuss his likes and dislikes with his tutor to help him incorporate his specific interests into his course. After finishing with a good grade, Mathew was able to progress onto his games design course and expand his love of computer games design and digital art due to the support of his tutor and the personalisation his course allowed. Mathew eventually applied for higher education to study his passion further.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/ua/ua_u2_s2_p2_casestudy.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
