          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>BETH: All individuals with autism have a unique set of strengths and abilities and to help them reach their full potential it is important to identify them. Once identified working with these strengths can be a way of helping improve their general motivation and development.</p>
<p>Some individuals with autism will be very driven by their particular skills and interests and if these are utilised appropriately, they can increase motivation.</p>
<p>let’s look at some examples of strengths commonly associated with autism and especially individuals that are high functioning.</p>
<p>JOE:</p>
<ul>
<li>Specialist knowledge and a high motivation in topics and activities that are of interest.</li>
<li>Exceptional memory for facts and figures and the ability to carry out mathematical tasks mentally with a high degree of accuracy.</li>
<li>An innovative approach to problem solving.</li>
<li>Innate skills in creative arts, such as Art and Music and use of memory to enhance this.</li>
<li>Enthusiasm for favourite interests with a drive to share this enjoyment with others.</li>
<li>Good almost obsessive attention to detail and ability to follow instructions especially when taught correctly.</li>
<li>A tendency to be honest and plain talking</li>
<li>Ability to see the world from a different perspective and can bring a different insight to things</li>
</ul>
<p>BETH: By working with and supporting the individual to bolster their strengths and abilities, a person with Autism should be enabled to reach their potential and focus on what motivates them, and in turn this should improve their confidence and self-worth in all aspects of their life.</p>

